A||||5||wkayl||Intermittent no spark…||||1||
Z||000000||wkayl||09-12-2001||12:33 PM||||A couple of times in the last 2 weeks my daughter's 88 Formula wouldn't start.  I replaced the coil and dist (having a spare engine sitting in the garage is handy).  I have unplugged the tach sensor at one time when it wouldn't start but it made no difference.  Last evening she went to drive it and it would only crank.  I told her I would work on it today (day off).  I went out and it stared right up after only turning over twice.  Any suggestions?<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.jpg">    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/usa1017[1].gif">   <BR>Dnyee's 88 Formula, Casey's 86GT, Wayne's 86GT.  <BR>We consider my wife as being "Fiero Challenged"||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-12-2001||02:24 PM||||Were you able to verify that the no-start was due to no spark?  Checked for stored codes? ||||reg||5
Z||000002||87GTZ34||09-12-2001||04:19 PM||||I had a similar problem when I first bought my first GT.  The problem turned out to be that the the two-wire harness from the coil to the distributor had an internal break in it and thats why the intermittant no spark on mine.  It is hard to diagnose as there was no break in the wire casing only internal.  I ended up vigorously 'bending & shaking' all of the wires around the coil/distributor when the car was running to find it.  I figured it was either that or the ignition switch.  Hope this helps.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick86GT||09-12-2001||06:30 PM||||I had a 2.8 with similar symptoms....turned out to be the distributor itself, likely the winding within.  Interestingly, the first second,and third rebuilt distributors I bought wouldn't start the engine (yes, 3 bad ones in a row from AutoZone) <P>The FOURTH one not only started the engine but solved the original problem.<P>BTW, I need a tach condensor if you want to sell your spare one.<P>Pat||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiero_24||09-12-2001||06:35 PM||||Mine was the ignition module.  The car died and would only crank, then I replaced the module and the car fired right up.<P>Craig<P>------------------<BR>  [<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||09-12-2001||10:14 PM||||My intermittent starting problem was cured by replacing the distributor pickup coil.  It finally gave up after 165,000 miles.  Some people replace it every time they do plugs, but I think that's a bit extreme, unless you run platinums and go years on a set.<BR>Pickup coil is under $10 at most parts places, just kind of a PITA to change.  The Fiero Online Service guide gives a good how to. <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/</A> ||||reg||1
